# Nexoris Technologies Digital Platform: environment template.
#
# Copy to .env and fill in real values. Never commit a real .env. Every value below is a safe
# placeholder. This file lists only variables the code actually reads: if a name is not here, the
# platform does not look it up.
#
# ====================================================================================================
# WHERE EACH VALUE GOES
# ====================================================================================================
# The repo root .env is what the scripts and the Oge gateway read. The two Next.js apps read their
# own apps/admin/.env.local and apps/web/.env.local, because Next does not look above the app
# directory. A value needed by an app has to be in that app's file, not only here.
#
# ====================================================================================================
# LOCAL MACHINE vs THE VPS
# ====================================================================================================
# Postgres runs on the VPS. Only the database host changes between the two:
#
#   From a developer machine   ...@194.147.95.7:5435/...   (the VPS, over the network)
#   Deployed on the VPS        ...@127.0.0.1:5435/...      (the same box, over loopback)
#
# Use 127.0.0.1 rather than localhost on the VPS: localhost can resolve to ::1, and Postgres may
# only be listening on IPv4. Loopback also keeps the traffic off the network interface, so the
# port does not need to be exposed to the internet at all.
#
# Everything else keeps the same value in both places, except the origins in the per-app files,
# which move from http://localhost:PORT to the real https:// domains.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# General
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NODE_ENV=development

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PostgreSQL: one server, one database per app.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The custom CMS (content, categories, authors, media, redirects, pSEO). Read by the website too,
# which queries it directly rather than through an API.
DATABASE_URL_CMS=postgresql://nexoris:password@194.147.95.7:5435/nexoris_cms

# The Oge AI gateway: conversations, knowledge-base chunks, embeddings.
DATABASE_URL_OGE=postgresql://nexoris:password@194.147.95.7:5435/nexoris_oge

# The admin platform: staff, CRM, finance, HR, payroll, e-invoicing. Also written by the gateway
# when it records a lead, so the CRM is the single home for leads.
DATABASE_URL_ADMIN=postgresql://nexoris:password@194.147.95.7:5435/nexoris_admin

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AI providers. Resolved per model slot by apiKeyFor(); a slot with no key is skipped, so the
# fallback chain continues rather than failing.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two Gemini projects, so a rate limit on one does not stop the other. Project A serves
# visitor-facing calls, Project B the internal CMS and vision work.
GEMINI_API_KEY_PROJECT_A=
GEMINI_API_KEY_PROJECT_B=
MISTRAL_API_KEY=
GROQ_API_KEY=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared secrets. Generate each with: openssl rand -base64 32
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signs the admin session cookie. Changing it signs everyone out.
ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET=

# Authorises the admin to revalidate website routes after a publish.
REVALIDATION_SECRET=

# Authorises admin-to-gateway calls: knowledge-base reingest, editorial generation, alt text.
OGE_REINGEST_SHARED_SECRET=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Oge AI gateway
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where the gateway listens. The browser never calls it directly; the website proxies through its
# own /api/chat route, so the gateway needs no public port.
OGE_PORT=4000
OGE_GATEWAY_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4000

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Meilisearch (site search)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
MEILISEARCH_HOST=http://127.0.0.1:7700
MEILISEARCH_API_KEY=

# ====================================================================================================
# apps/admin/.env.local
# ====================================================================================================
# NODE_ENV, DATABASE_URL_ADMIN, DATABASE_URL_CMS, ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET, REVALIDATION_SECRET,
# OGE_GATEWAY_URL and OGE_REINGEST_SHARED_SECRET repeat from above, plus:
#
# APP_URL=https://app.nexoristech.com
#   This admin app's own origin. Invitation and password-reset links are built from it, so a wrong
#   value emails links that go nowhere. Local: http://localhost:3102
#
# WEB_ORIGIN=https://nexoristech.com
#   The public website, used to revalidate its routes on publish. Local: http://localhost:3000
#
# DATABASE_URL_OGE=
#   The admin reads this one table read-only, to report on the System tab how many pages Oge knows
#   and when they were last refreshed. Publishing in the CMS re-ingests automatically; the hardcoded
#   pages, where the address and the services live, are refreshed by hand with:
#     pnpm --filter @nexoris/web kb:export && pnpm --filter @nexoris/oge kb:ingest
#   Skipping that after a site edit is how Oge came to give visitors an office address the website
#   had stopped showing two months earlier.
#
# MEDIA_STORAGE_PATH=/media/nexoris
#   Where uploaded media is written. Leave unset locally and files stay in
#   apps/admin/public/uploads as before. Set it in production to a directory OUTSIDE the
#   application, or a deploy that replaces the app directory erases every uploaded file
#   while the database rows survive. The public URL stays /uploads/<name> either way.
#
# ADMIN_SETTINGS_KEY=
#   64 hex characters (32 bytes). Seals the credentials stored in the admin database, currently the
#   Mailjet API key and secret in email_settings, with AES-256-GCM. Generate one with:
#     node -e "console.log(require('node:crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
#   Required before email can be configured: without it the settings screen refuses to save a secret
#   rather than writing it in the clear. Losing it does not lose the platform, re-enter the credential
#   in Settings and it is sealed again; changing it invalidates every sealed value, which is rotation.
#
#   There is deliberately no MAILJET_API_KEY here. Who sends the platform's mail is configured at
#   Settings, Email Delivery, and stored sealed in the database, so there is one source of truth
#   instead of an environment variable and a settings screen that can disagree.
#
# GSC_PROPERTY=sc-domain:nexoristech.com
#   Prefer the Domain property form (sc-domain:) over a URL prefix. It covers www and non-www, http
#   and https, in one property, so it does not have to be changed if the canonical host ever does,
#   and Search Console grants it full permission rather than the restricted level a URL-prefix
#   property added by someone else tends to carry. The account that must be granted access is the
#   service account the platform authenticates as, not the person who owns the site.
# GA4_PROPERTY_ID=
#   Search Console property and the NUMERIC GA4 property id (not the G- measurement id).
#
# GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=
#   Where the Google credential lives. This is the piece that does NOT travel with a deployment, and
#   leaving it unset is why the SEO screens read "not connected" on a fresh server while working on
#   the machine they were built on.
#
#   The two names above say WHICH property to read. They are not credentials. The identity comes from
#   Application Default Credentials, which google-auth-library discovers from the host in this order:
#     1. a service account key file named by this variable,
#     2. the file `gcloud auth application-default login` writes into the user's own profile,
#     3. a Google-hosted metadata server (Cloud Run, GCE, Workload Identity Federation).
#
#   A developer's machine satisfies (2) after a one-off login, so nothing needs to be set here in
#   local development. A plain VPS is none of those things, so it needs (1): point this at a service
#   account key file kept outside the repository, readable only by the user running the admin.
#
#   The account to authenticate as is the one already granted access in Search Console and GA4 — a
#   service account, not a person. Grant it read access in Search Console (Settings, Users and
#   permissions) and Viewer on the GA4 property. Absent any credential the calls return null and the
#   screens say so, rather than showing invented figures.
# INDEXNOW_KEY=
#   Submits published URLs to IndexNow. Must match the key file the website serves.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=
#   Only a fallback for APP_URL above, kept because the name is conventional. Set APP_URL instead;
#   this one carries the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, so whatever it holds reaches the browser.
#
# ADMIN_SEED_EMAIL=
#   The address of the bootstrap administrator this deployment was seeded with, so Settings, Owner
#   can point at the right row and offer to retire it. It names an account; it is not a credential.
#
#   Unset it once that account is gone. The screen says so itself, and a value naming an account
#   that no longer exists leaves the owner screen describing a seeded account nobody can find.
#
# ADMIN_SEED_PASSWORD=
# ADMIN_SEED_NAME=
#   Read only by scripts/seed-admin.ts, which creates the first administrator so somebody can sign
#   in to a fresh deployment. ADMIN_SEED_PASSWORD is a real credential and the only one in this file
#   that is.
#
#   All three ADMIN_SEED_* variables must be removed from the deployment once the first
#   administrator has signed in and set their own password. Left in place they are a working
#   credential sitting in the environment of a running production system, and the seed script is
#   idempotent on email, so a deploy that still carries them can recreate an account the owner
#   deliberately retired.
#
# KB_FILE=
#   Overrides where apps/oge/scripts/ingest.ts reads the knowledge base from. Defaults to
#   artifacts/knowledge-base.json at the repository root. Tooling only; no runtime effect.
#
# SEO_MANIFEST=
#   Overrides the manifest path the check:seo gate validates. Defaults to the one the web build
#   writes. Tooling only.
#
# WEB_VERIFY_ORIGIN=
#   The origin scripts/verify-public-web.mjs checks. Defaults to http://localhost:3000; point it at
#   the deployed site to run the same checks against production. Tooling only.

# ====================================================================================================
# apps/web/.env.local
# ====================================================================================================
# NODE_ENV, DATABASE_URL_CMS, OGE_GATEWAY_URL, REVALIDATION_SECRET, MEILISEARCH_HOST and
# INDEXNOW_KEY repeat from above, plus:
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GA4_MEASUREMENT_ID=G-XXXXXXXXXX
#   The G- measurement id. NEXT_PUBLIC_ means it reaches the browser, which is correct here and is
#   why the numeric property id above must never carry that prefix.
#
# CMS_MEDIA_BASE=https://media.nexoristech.com
#   Base URL that CMS-uploaded media is served from, set on the WEBSITE (apps/web).
#
#   Uploads are written into the admin app's public/uploads and served by the admin origin. The
#   website is a different origin, so without this every CMS image resolves against the website,
#   which has no such file: article covers, author headshots and in-body pictures all render as
#   broken images with the alt text showing. Nothing errors, which is what makes it easy to miss.
#
#   In development that means http://localhost:3102 (the admin dev server). In production it is
#   wherever the uploads directory is actually served from. Leave blank only if the website and the
#   media directory genuinely share an origin.
#
# CMS_ALLOW_EMPTY_BUILD=1
#   Optional. The build fails on purpose if the CMS returns nothing, so a database outage cannot
#   quietly ship a site with no content. Set this only to build deliberately against an empty CMS.

# ====================================================================================================
# NRS e-invoicing (apps/admin). Leave unset until the SI/APP integration is commissioned.
# ====================================================================================================
# Fiscalisation fails closed while these are unset, which is the intended state: no invoice is
# marked fiscalised without a real acceptance from the service.
#
# The cryptographic key is never sent to the browser, never logged, never returned by an API and
# never displayed after configuration. Business ID and Service ID are separate values; do not
# assume one equals the other.
#
# NRS_SIAPP_ENDPOINT=
# NRS_SIAPP_BUSINESS_ID=
# NRS_SIAPP_SERVICE_ID=
# NRS_SIAPP_CRYPTO_KEY=
